so I suppose if I go back to the  beginning it I learned about this time  last year when it was first announced  that Kate was pregnant that I would be  covering the royal birth so I was very  excited about that and I did an awful  lot of research ahead of time so I knew  what would happen where she was going to  have the baby I knew what was going to  happen with other people that were  having a baby on the same day and you  about the christening gown and you about  potential godparents everything I could  possibly cram into my little head what I  didn't know was when she was going to go  into labor and so from the beginning of  July when it was sometime in July so  from the beginning of July I didn't move  very far from central London at all and  I had a bag packed at the front door  waiting for the call from the news desk  to say Kate's in labor labor can you  head down to the Lindo wing I was more  prepared for this birth than I was for  my own so I had some cream  I had kugels I had autocue I had  breakfast bars  I had Diet Coke I had my notebook I had  my flat shoes I had a change of clothes  I had everything I could possibly need  and then at 7 o'clock that morning I got  the call saying it appears that she's  gone into labor somebody's seen her  going into the hospital with William and  so that was around about 20 plus 7 I was  there at 8 o'clock to go live on air and  then I broadcast live more or less all  the time apart from a couple of hours  sleep in the middle until we got the  news at around about half-past eight the  following night and then I finished on  our about nine o'clock and I went home  and had the biggest glass of wine you've  ever seen the atmosphere down on the  ground was electric actually everybody  got on incredibly well even though it  was so busy and packed and there were  hundreds of photographers and cameramen  and reporters from all around the world  I mean this was a global occasion so I  just had to squeeze in tight and turn to  one side and could just about still see  the camera but everybody got on  incredibly well as I said and we had an  agreement with the photographers so it  was the cameras first and then the  photographer's in banks on ladders  moving back from them we agreed that  once they came out or indeed any of the  Royals going in or  out all the Middletons we would crouch  down so that they could get a good shot  but on occasion although we were trying  to fill air we would take a commercial  break every now and again and the camera  and would be throwing Percy pigs at us  and we would have a bit of a food fight  you know it was a really fabulous  occasion and when we got the news that  the baby had been born four hours  earlier and we hadn't we hadn't even had  an inkling and that's when everything  erupted it's it's a son we're being told  Anna it's a sunbonnet for 24 p.m. there  were 36 hours when we thought any moment  were going to hear the news that the  baby has been born finally we heard  after about 12 hours or so we finally  heard that the baby had been born and  then we thought potentially she's had a  healthy happy birth at the baby and the  royal couple would come out that night  and go back to Kensington Palace but  when they close those Lindo wing doors  then we knew that wasn't going to happen  so we went and got a quick bit of Kip  and came back the day after and at any  stage that day they could have come out  and so we were literally on tenterhooks  the whole time then we got the  announcement in fact it was Charles who  had noticed that we were there and said  all you've been here a long time and  then when he came out a few minutes  later we thought that was really quick  and we asked him we shouted to him you  know how is everybody and he said you'll  find out in a minute and we thought it's  going to be neither babies coming out so  from that moment we crouched down as  we'd agreed with with the photographers  i sat in a puddle with my white jeans on  had a very wet bottom and sat there for  an hour an hour and a half waiting for  that moment  but eventually when they did come out  and we saw him and he almost did a  little regal wave didn't he as he was  stretching his arms up Prince George  Louis Allen Prince George Alexander  Louie isn't it  he was a whopper what a great big baby  he was but it was all worth it for just  that moment it was a moment in history  and I was there how cool is that  we saw William putting the car seat into  the car Kate got into the back and he  sort of clicked it in like an expert but  he gave it away when he clicked it in  close the door and was just about to get  into the driver's seat in he went and  then admitted later but he had been  sweating it and he had been practicing  for a very long time to try to make sure  that moment went without a hitch and  indeed it did I was incredibly lucky to  have a ringside seat in that moment in  history when a future heir to the throne  was introduced to the world lucky me
 

